   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public lands law, in relation to the transfer of\nunappropriated state lands for mental health, developmental disability, park,\nrecreation, playground, reforestation, public education, public safety, street\nor highway purposes",
    "basePrintNo": "S8977",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8977-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 5,
       "fullName": "Jim Gaughran",
       "imgName": "1042_Jim_Gaughran.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 1222,
       "sessionMemberId": 1807,
       "sessionYear": 2021,
       "shortName": "GAUGHRAN"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §34, Pub Lds L",
    "lawSection": "Public Lands Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8977\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public lands law, in relation to the transfer of\nunappropriated state lands for mental health, developmental disability,\npark, recreation, playground, reforestation, public education, public\nsafety, street or highway purposes\n\n1.  \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend Section 34 of the Public Lands Law\nto expand the types of local government bodies that may obtain surplus\nState lands for nominal consideration and to expand the purposes for\nwhich local governments may use such surplus State land.\n\n2.  \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThis bill amends Section 34 of the Public Lands Law by (i) adding poli-\ntical subdivisions, fire companies, and volunteer ambulance services, as\nthose terms are defined in Section 100 of the General Municipal Law, to\nthe list of local government bodies that may obtain surplus State lands\nfor nominal consideration and (ii) adding public education and public\nsafety purposes to the list of purposes for which such surplus State\nproperty must be used by local governments.  The bill also makes a tech-\nnical amendment by updating the phrase \"mental retardation\" to \"develop-\nmental disability.\"\n\n3.  \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nCurrent law authorizes the Office of General Services (\"OGS\") to trans-\nfer and convey to a city, incorporated village, town, or county, in\nconsideration of $1.00, unappropriated, lands for local mental health\nfacilities, mental retardation facilities, park, recreation, playground,\nreforestation, street or highway purposes only. Current law also\nmandates that the deed of conveyance contain a reverter to the State if\nthe property is not used for the designated purpose.\n\n4.  \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill. In 2018 and 2021, OGS proposed similar bills that\nwould have also allowed for the transfer of State surplus property for\n\"other municipal purposes.\" In 2018, that proposal became an Art VII.\nbill in the Executive Budget, but it was ultimately omitted during Budg-\net negotiations. In 2021, that proposal was advanced to the Executive\nChamber as a departmental bill, but it did not advance any further. The\ncurrent proposal is more limited by not including those broader\npurposes.\n\n5.  \nSTATEMENT IN SUPPORT:\n\nUnder current law, the OGS may transfer surplus State lands for $1.00 to\na city, village, town, or county as long as the land will be used for\nlocal mental health facilities, mental retardation facilities, park,\nrecreation, playground, reforestation, street, or highway purposes. Over\nthe years, this law has been a major benefit to both the State and local\ngovernments, allowing the State to shed the expense of maintaining\n\nunused State real property and allowing municipalities to obtain needed\nproperty for free and put it into a productive reuse.\n\nUnfortunately, the scope of Section 34 is narrow, and there have been\nmany instances when a municipality has identified a piece of surplus\nState real property that would suit the needs of the municipality but\nthe proposed use does not fit neatly within any of the purposes set\nforth in Section 34. In these cases, the municipality must either\npurchase the real property from the State for valuable consideration or\nseek special legislation authorizing the State to convey the real prop-\nerty to the municipality for $1.00. Such legislation always contains a\nuse restriction and a reverter provision.\n\nUnder current law, only cities, villages, towns, and counties may obtain\nsurplus State real property for nominal consideration, but other kinds\nof governmental bodies have needs that are just as urgent. This bill\nwould expand the eligible entities to three types of entities that are\ndefined in Section 100 of the General Municipal Law: political subdivi-\nsions (which, in addition to municipal corporations, includes school\ndistricts and boards of cooperative educational services), fire compa-\nnies, and voluntary ambulance services. These types of governmental\nbodies provide important public services and should have the benefit of\neasier access to suitable surplus State real property.\n\nThe amendments to Section 34 will facilitate the conveyance of parcels\nof unappropriated State land that are suitable for local government\npurposes, but which do not fit into the distinct categories of the\ncurrent statute. When State lands are declared to be surplus, the\nCommissioner of General Services is required by Public Lands Law § 30-a\nto provide notice to the chief elected officials of the county and the\nmunicipality where the lands are located. Such notice sometimes prompts\nan expression of interest in the property from local governments, which\nthe Commissioner takes into account when reviewing how best to dispose\nof surplus property.\n\nWhen local governments are interested in surplus State real property,\nwhether it be vacant land or improved property, the proposed reuse of\nthe property may involve various governmental purposes (e.g., educa-\ntional facilities, fire houses, public safety facilities, jails, or\nother similar uses) that are not permitted under the current statute.\nBecause of these limitations in the current law, local governments often\nencounter significant delays and uncertainty in seeking special legis-\nlation. Such unnecessary delays also cause State agencies to incur\nsignificant additional carrying costs to maintain the surplus property,\nparticularly in the case of improved real property where heat and secu-\nrity must be provided.\n\nThe proposed changes to Section 34 will address limitations in the\nexisting law by expanding the types of local government bodies that can\nobtain surplus State property for nominal consideration and by expanding\nthe purposes to include a broader range of local government uses. These\nchanges will not modify the safeguards that are built into the existing\nlaw, which provide that the local government must improve or maintain\nthe property for the permitted purposes, or upon failure to do so, the\nlands shall revert to the People of the State of New York.\n\n6.  \nBUDGET IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill would reduce State expenditures for the maintenance and secu-\nrity of State surplus property.\n\n7.  \nLOCAL IMPACT:\n\nThis bill would make more State-owned surplus real property available to\nlocal governments at nominal consideration.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
